[.lesson-title]
SUSIE SUNBEAM.

. Susie Sunbeam was not her real
name; that was Susan Brown. But every
// page 31
one called her Susie Sunbeam, because
she had such a sweet, smiling face,
and always brought brightness with her
when she came.

image::image0028.png["Older girls playing with younger girl. Three children standing in background.",pdfwidth=252.9pt,pdfheight=185.3pt]

[start=2]
. Her grandfather first gave her this
name, and it seemed to fit the little girl
so nicely that soon it took the place of
her own.
. Even when a baby, Susie laughed
and crowed from morning till night.
No one ever heard her cry unless she
was sick or hurt.
. When she had learned to walk,
// page 32
she loved to go about the house and
get things for her mother, and in this
way save her as many steps as she
could.
. She would sit by her mother’s
side for an hour at a time, and ask
her ever so many questions, or she
would take her new book and read.
. Susie was always pleasant in her
play with other children. She never
used an unkind word, but tried to do
whatever would please her playmates
best.
. One day, a poor little girl with a
very ragged dress was going by, and
Susie heard some children teasing her
and making fun of her.
. She at once ran out to the gate,
and asked the poor little girl to come
in. “What are you crying for?” Susie
asked.
. “Because they all laugh at me,”
she said.
. Then Susie took the little girl into
the house. She cheered her up with
// page 33
kind words, and gave her a nice dress
and a pair of shoes.
. This brought real joy and glad{shy}ness
to the poor child, and she, too,
thought that Susie was rightly called
Sunbeam.

