[.lesson-title]
THE BEE.

. Bees live in a house that is called
a hive. They are of three kinds,—work{shy}ers,
drones, and queens.
. Only one queen can live in each
hive. If she is lost or dead, the other
bees will stop their work.
// page 47

image::image0042.png["Three bee-hives; wooden boxes about two feet square and four feet high, with a sloped roof.",pdfwidth=257.6pt,pdfheight=229.6pt]

[start=3]
. They are very wise and busy little
creatures. They all join together to build
cells of wax for their honey.
. Each bee takes its proper place, and
does its own work. Some go out and
gather honey from the flowers; others
stay at home and work inside the hive.
. The cells which they build, are all
of one shape and size, and no room is
left between them.
// page 48
. The cells are not round, but have
six sides.
. Did you ever look into a glass
hive to see the bees while at work?
It is pleasant to see how busy they
always are.
. But the drones do not work. Be{shy}fore
winter comes, all the drones are
driven from the hive or killed, that they
may not eat the honey which they did
not gather.
. It is not quite safe for children to
handle bees. They have sharp stings
that they know well how to use in their
defense.

[.slate]
SLATE WORK.

[.script.script-verse,at=384]
How doth the little busy bee
{em}Improve each shining hour,
And gather honey all the day
{em}From every opening flower!
